@aojea newer patch works.

For those asking what Antonio's patch does, it "basically" (between quotes, because take a look into the PR!) creates the flannel.1 interface with tx and rx offloading off.

So this 'automates' the workaround.

@aojea thanks for looking into this.

Can you highlight the performance implications associated with turning tx and rx offloading off on the flannel interface. It seems like the performance implications can be high for high throughput environments: 1gbps, 10gbps, 40gbps. My application is quite high throughput.

The nominal 1bps, per CPU clock cycle that you see ("Conclusion and Future Work") [here](http://www.nanogrids.org/jaidev/papers/ispass03.pdf), is what drives people towards hardware based offloading. I am trying to understand how flannel ties into this "offloading to buy-back CPU cycles" goal.

@danwinship @rikatz I don't think option 4 will work for myself or @jhohertz at the very least - we're both using Flatcar Linux, and I can confirm that the latest stable version with kernel 4.19.124-flatcar did not fix this issue in my cluster.

Personally I'd be fine with option 5 since `--random-fully` just appears completely broken on my cluster and I barely use NodePort services at all, much less the way they're used as described in #76699.

I think option 2 should be given some serious thought though; given that this issue still exists on some systems despite having a recent kernel with the checksum fix, it seems like there are other factors that determine the impact of using `--random-fully`.

@therealbubbasour glad to see you found this issue.. @rikatz and others have done an excellent job of consolidating many disparate reports that relate to this, under this. I've been monitoring the progress all along. I feel that we are close. 

I found that I spent a lot of time up front isolating the problem and eliminating elements. It took me a lot of learning about k8s, to finally get to the point where I knew I had found and reasonably isolated that crucial problem.

Stay tuned.

@raz3k Having both legacy and nft rules it's a problem. From the nftables [wiki](https://wiki.nftables.org/wiki-nftables/index.php/Moving_from_iptables_to_nftables):
> Beware of using both the nft and the legacy tools at the same time. That means using both x_tables and nf_tables kernel subsystems at the same time, and could lead to unexpected results.

I am not sure the [script](https://github.com/kubernetes/release/blob/master/images/build/debian-iptables/buster/iptables-wrapper) that detects the iptables flavour correctly in this case. There is a case there where in the end it defaults to legacy if no rules exist.
